om_str::<Value>` at `crates/c3/src/consult/ingest.rs:199` represents an object as a map and retains only the last duplicate; the later `serde_json::from_value` at `crates/c3/src/consult/ingest.rs:205` therefore sees only `verdict:\"ACCEPT\"`. The contradictory first value is silently discarded and the malformed reply becomes a valid structured reply. This is separate from object selection and requires duplicate-key rejection before conversion to `Value`.\n\nThe control-character state machine is otherwise correct: it toggles string state on unescaped quotes, consumes the character after a backslash through `esc`, and escapes only raw U+0000..U+001F characters while in a string (`crates/c3/src/consult/ingest.rs:253-285`). This correctly handles odd/even runs of backslashes and escaped quotes. Malformed backslash sequences are left for `serde_json` to reject rather than being silently completed.\n\n## 2. Acceptance of garbage\n\nExact input:\n\n```text\nThis is a fenced example, not the review:\n```json\n{\"schema_version\":\"1\",\"reply_markdown\":\"banana\",\"verdict\":\"ADVISE\",\"verdict_reason\":\"sample\",\"findings\":[],\"prior_findings\":[],\"unproven\":[],\"first_run_checklist\":[]}\n```\nWhat does it mean?\n```\n\nThe whole-response fence strip does not match because prose precedes the fence (`crates/c3/src/consult/ingest.rs:149-158,178-180`). `outermost_object` then ignores the fence and prose, starts at the example object's opening brace, and extracts it (`crates/c3/src/consult/ingest.rs:219,235-247`). That object has the same complete shape as the known accepted object in the test at `crates/c3/src/consult/ingest.rs:351-353`; after the defaulting and array checks it validates through `StructuredReply::try_from` at `crates/c3/src/consult/ingest.rs:204-207`. Thus an explicitly labelled illustrative example is promoted to the review of record.\n\nThe fix is to make extraction candidate-aware: ignore objects inside nested Markdown fences or code spans, collect all balanced top-level candidates, and unwrap only when exactly one non-code candidate parses successfully. Multiple candidates, a candidate inside an inner code block, or a brace located in a non-whole fence should leave the response invalid rather than choosing one.\n\n## 3. Non-determinism or non-idempotence\n\nNo such defect was found in the deterministic repair steps. The scans are left-to-right over the input, and the object, string, and escape states are updated deterministically at `crates/c3/src/consult/ingest.rs:217-285`. Array wrapping changes an object/string/null once into an already conforming array, and numeric schema version `1` becomes string `\"1\"`; later passes do not repeat either change (`crates/c3/src/consult/ingest.rs:290-343`).\n\nInterpreting `normalise(normalise(x))` as serialising the returned `StructuredReply` and passing that JSON back into `normalise_reply`, the emitted object is semantically idempotent: control characters are already escaped, fields are already arrays, and `schema_version` is already `\"1\"`. The duplicate-key case is idempotent only because its meaning was already destroyed on the first pass. The existing test reruns the same already-valid source rather than the serialised result of a repaired input (`crates/c3/src/consult/ingest.rs:453-465`), so dynamic confirmation for every repaired shape remains unproven, but code inspection shows no non-determinism or second-pass semantic change.\n\n## 4. Cost\n\nNo quadratic-or-worse path was found for `normalise_reply`. `outermost_object` performs one search and one linear scan, with one size-proportional slice allocation (`crates/c3/src/consult/ingest.rs:217-247`). `escape_control_chars_in_strings` makes one character pass and produces at most a constant-factor larger string (`crates/c3/src/consult/ingest.rs:253-285`). JSON parsing/conversion and structural validation are also traversed once, while structural repair does constant work per top-level field or finding (`crates/c3/src/consult/ingest.rs:198-206,307-324`). A multi-megabyte input therefore has linear scan/allocation work, though no wall-clock or peak-memory benchmark was supplied.",
