C:\Users\Dmytro\C3\crates\c3\tests\fixtures\http\03-http-reply.reply.json:2:  "reply_markdown": "# Security Review: HTTP Engine Key-to-Host Binding\n\n## Summary\n\nThe validation logic in `roster_ext.rs` is **largely sound** for its core security property: preventing a known provider key (e.g., `OPENAI_API_KEY`) from being sent to a host other than that provider's domain or a subdomain thereof. The `url` crate is used correctly for parsing, the subdomain check is correct, and the `C3_KEY_` escape hatch works as designed.\n\nHowever, **three concrete secret-leakage bugs** exist in error-message formatting, and one **header-value validation gap** allows non-string header values to be accepted into the roster (though they are later rejected with leakage).\n\n## Findings by Category\n\n### 1. Exfiltration — Key-to-Host Binding\n\n**Verdict: HOLDS** — no bypass found.\n\n- `parse_base_url` (lines 121–154) uses `url::Url::parse()`, which normalizes the host (lowercases, strips trailing dot, decodes IDNA/punycode to ASCII). The returned `host_str()` is the canonical host without port, userinfo, or path.\n- `check_key_host` (lines 160–179) lowercases the host again (idempotent) and checks `host == bound || host.ends_with(\".{bound}\")`. This correctly accepts `api.openai.com` and `eu.api.openai.com` but rejects `api.openai.com.evil.example`, `evilopenrouter.ai`, and `openrouter.ai.evil.example` (tested at lines 735–740).\n- IP literals (`https://1.2.3.4`) yield host `\"1.2.3.4\"`, which matches no known bound domain → rejected.\n- The `C3_KEY_` prefix rule (line 168) requires at least one character after the prefix (`len > 7`), so `C3_KEY_` alone is rejected (tested line 759).\n- **No parsing differential** between validator and `ureq`: both use the `url` crate. The validator stores the original URL string (line 377) and re-parses it once for the host (line 391); `ureq` will parse the same string at request time.\n\n### 2. Header Injection / Smuggling\n\n**Verdict: HOLDS** — no injection vector found.\n\n- Header names are validated by `header_name_problem` (lines 183–195) against `RESERVED_HEADERS` (case-insensitive) and RFC 7230 `tchar` via `is_http_token` (lines 198–220). The token set matches the RFC exactly.\n- Header values are checked only for CR/LF (lines 448–452). RFC 7230 allows any VCHAR/obs-text; `ureq` will percent-encode or reject invalid bytes. No smuggling vector via values alone.\n- Reserved headers (`Authorization`, `Host`, `Cookie`, etc.) are blocked (line 107–115), so the engine's own `Authorization: Bearer <key>` and `Host` cannot be overridden.\n\n### 3. Validator vs. HTTP Client Interpretation Differences\n\n**Verdict: HOLDS** — no difference found.\n\n- Both validator and `ureq` 2.x use the `url` crate for parsing. The validator extracts `host_str()` for the binding check; `ureq` uses the full URL for the request. The host component is identical.\n- The validator permits a path (e.g., `/api/v1`); `ureq` will append the request path (`/chat/completions`) to it. This is by design (line 119: \"a port and a path are allowed\").\n\n### 4. Secret Leakage in Refusal Messages\n\n**Verdict: THREE LEAKS FOUND** — error messages serialize user-supplied JSON values via `compact()` (lines 256–258), which calls `serde_json::to_string()`. If a roster author (or prompt-injected agent) places a secret in a header **value** that is not a string, or in the `headers` field as a non-object, or in `base_url` as a non-string, the secret appears verbatim in the refusal string returned to the caller (and potentially logged).\n\n| Location | Trigger | Leaked Value |
C:\Users\Dmytro\C3\crates\c3\tests\fixtures\http\03-http-reply.reply.json:4:| `roster_ext.rs:441` | Header value is not a JSON string (e.g., `{\"X-Key\": {\"secret\": \"sk-...\"}}`) | Entire JSON value via `compact(val)` |
C:\Users\Dmytro\C3\crates\c3\tests\fixtures\http\03-http-reply.reply.json:5:| `roster_ext.rs:431` | `headers` field is not an object (e.g., `\"headers\": {\"Authorization\": \"Bearer sk-...\"}`) | Entire `headers` value via `compact(hv)` |
C:\Users\Dmytro\C3\crates\c3\tests\fixtures\http\03-http-reply.reply.json:6:| `roster_ext.rs:382` | `base_url` field is not a string (e.g., `\"base_url\": {\"url\": \"https://evil.com\", \"token\": \"...\"}`) | Entire `base_url` value via `compact(bv)` |
C:\Users\Dmytro\C3\crates\c3\tests\fixtures\http\03-http-reply.reply.json:8:The `key_env` field (line 400) only leaks the **variable name**, not its value — correct.\n\n## Recommended Fixes\n\n1. **Replace `compact()` in error paths with a safe summarizer** that never includes string contents (e.g., show JSON type only: `\"object\"`, `\"number\"`, `\"string\"`).\n2. **Validate header values are strings earlier** (before the loop) and refuse non-strings with a generic message.\n3. **Validate `base_url` and `headers` types before any `compact()` call** — already done for `base_url` (line 375) but the error path at 382 still uses `compact`; move the type check before the parse attempt.\n4. **Consider redacting header values in all error messages** as a defense-in-depth measure.\n\n## Additional Notes\n\n- The `C3_KEY_` escape hatch is working as designed: any variable named `C3_KEY_<SUFFIX>` (with non-empty suffix) may be sent to any HTTPS host. This is a **user-controlled** variable; prompt injection cannot create environment variables, only roster entries.\n- No findings for prior open issues (none listed).\n- Unproven: behavior of `ureq` with extremely long header values, or with Unicode in header values (RFC 7230 allows only ASCII tokens for names; values may contain obs-text). The validator does not limit header value length.",
